    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d393">

                <pb facs="0579" n="503" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg503"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.00/911: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in Greece</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">MacVeagh</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-10-15T18:00:00-05:00">October 15, 1935—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>68. Your 156, October 14, 2 p.m. As you report that your colleagues, and
                    particularly the representatives of the protecting powers under the Treaty of
                    London of May 7, 1832, are replying to the Foreign Minister’s note in the usual
                    manner, the Department perceives no reason why you should not do the same.</p>
                <p>For background information to this instruction please see Department’s telegram
                    No. 63, April 16, 6 p.m., 1924.<anchor n="15" xml:id="d393-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d393-f1" n="15" xml:id="d393-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram No. 63, April 16, 1924, 6 p.m., was from the Chargé in Greece. The
                    Department’s instruction in reply was telegram No. 46, April 17, 1924, 5 p.m.
                    See <ref target="frus1924v02#pg_271"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations</hi>, 1924, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, pp.
                        271</ref>, <ref target="frus1924v02#pg_272">272</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
